ucture from rel01.0. Looking at the sponge_soak_before_write test case more carefully, I see the critical contract it's testing: when piping through sponge, the output file shouldn't be truncated if sponge opens it before consuming all stdin. In the test harness, I'd simulate this by creating a data file, then running sponge with that content as stdin and verifying the output matches what was read in.\n\nThe differential test approach is simpler—I just need to confirm the Go binary produces identical output to the reference binary with the same inputs, which is sufficient even if it doesn't perfectly stress the soak-before-write guarantee. I'm overthinking the implementation details here. Let me focus on writing the actual task, keeping in mind the constraint that I can only define one task and should prioritize the earliest incomplete release.\n\nLooking at the roadmap, rel01.0 is already in progress with the ts use case implemented, so the clear next deliverable is sponge_test.go for rel01.1 since the main.go files for wc, cat, and sponge are all done but sponge's tests are missing.\n\nNow I need to structure the test file carefully, grounding my design decisions in the PRDs rather than making arbitrary choices. The key constraints are that file-state comparison applies to utilities whose primary output is a file, differential tests should compare output file content after both binaries exit, and each test case needs to follow the preconditions from test-rel01.1.yaml.\n\nI'm also anchoring to the reference binary being \"sponge\" from moreutils rather than a g-prefixed variant, and following the TestMain pattern I see in the existing cmd/cat and cmd/wc test files. The PRD specifies that tests must cover small stdin, large stdin, append mode, passthrough mode, missing output files, existing output files with mode preservation, and cross-device rename fallback scenarios.\n\nNow I'm realizing the key insight: the harness needs to compare the Go binary's output file against what the reference binary actually produces, not against hardcoded expected content. The FileExpectation utility provides byte-for-byte file comparison, which is exactly what R6.1 requires—the test should run both binaries and verify their output files match. I see there's a tension between R5, which expects static file content specifications, and R6, which requires comparing output files between the Go and reference binaries. The current harness handles R5 but not the full R6 comparison. For the sponge tests, I'll use RunDiffTests for passthrough mode and create a custom helper that runs each binary in isolated directories to compare their output files directly.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the task itself. Given the constraint that agents need complete independence with just the task description and execution constitution, I need to provide clear guidance for handling file-output differential tests. Looking at the scope—around 250-350 lines—a sponge_test.go file with TestMain, a helper function for file-output comparisons, and eight test cases should fit well within that budget. I'm reviewing the test utilities and reference implementations to understand the harness API, normalization functions, and how to structure the differential tests, then I'll create the sponge_test.go file following the established patterns from the cat and wc test suites while implementing the TestMain setup and test case execution for all eight sponge scenarios. the Go sponge binary into a temp directory via \"go build -o <path> .\", locate the Homebrew reference binary \"sponge\" (brew install moreutils) via exec.LookPath(\"sponge\"), and set package-level goBinary and refBinary variables. Clean up the temp directory after m.Run(). Per prd007-sponge R6 and sources.yaml (moreutils utilities are not g-prefixed).\n\nFor the runFileDiffTest helper, I need to handle the fact that sponge writes to files rather than stdout. The approach is to create two isolated temp directories with identical fixture files, run the Go binary in one directory and the reference binary in the other with the same stdin and arguments, then compare stdout, stderr, exit codes, and the output file contents byte-for-byte from both runs. The test report should include the arguments, truncated stdin (256 bytes max), both file contents, both stdouts, both stderrs, and both exit codes to help diagnose any divergence. Per prd007-sponge R6.1 and prd001-testutils R5.1-R5.2.\n\nFor the sponge_small_stdin_to_file test, I'm generating 100 lines of input (\"1\\n\" through \"100\\n\") and piping it as stdin to sponge with the output file argument, then comparing the resulting file content between the two binaries. This traces prd007-sponge R1.1, R1.2, and R2.1 per test-rel01.1.yaml.\n\nNow I'm setting up the sponge_soak_before_write test case, which creates a fixture file containing three lines in both temp directories, runs each binary with that same content as stdin and the fixture file as the target, then compares the output file content. both directories. The file must not be empty or truncated, proving sponge soaked stdin before opening the output. Per test-rel01.1.yaml sponge_soak_before_write, tracing prd007-sponge R1.1, R2.5.\n      - id: R5\n        text: |\n          Implement the sponge_passthrough_stdout test case using RunDiffTests (standard stdout\n          comparison). stdin is \"hello world\\n\", args is empty (no output filename). Both binaries\n          should write stdin content to stdout. Per test-rel01.1.yaml sponge_passthrough_stdout,\n          tracing prd007-sponge R4.1, R4.3.\n      - id: R6\n        text: |\n          For append mode tests, I need to set up a fixture file with \"original line\\n\" and run both binaries with stdin \"appended line\\n\" and the -a flag, verifying the output contains both lines. When the file doesn't exist, running with -a and new content should create it with just that content. Per test-rel01.1.yaml, tracing prd007-sponge R3.1-R3.3.\n      - id: R7\n        text: |\n          For the large stdin test, I'm generating a payload exceeding 1,048,576 bytes and piping it to both binaries writing to large_out.txt, then comparing the output files to ensure the temp-file spill mechanism works correctly. Per test-rel01.1.yaml sponge_large_stdin, tracing prd007-sponge R1.3, R1.4.\n      - id: R8\n        text: |\n          For the file mode preservation test, I need to create a fixture file with mode 0640, run both binaries with new stdin content, and verify both the file content and the mode are preserved at 0640. Per test-rel01.1.yaml sponge_output_file_exists_mode, tracing prd007-sponge R2.1, R2.2.\n      - id: R9\n        text: |\n          Testing empty stdin handling by passing zero bytes to both binaries writing to empty_out.txt, then confirming the output file is empty in both directories. Per test-rel01.1.yaml sponge_empty_stdin, tracing prd007-sponge R1.1, R2.1.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          The differential test framework must compare the output file content after both binaries exit, not stdout. Since both binaries run in the same working directory, I need to handle potential conflicts carefully.\n\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          For file-output tests, I'll create isolated temp directories for each binary with identical fixtures, run them separately, then compare the resulting output files. For passthrough tests that check stdout, I'll use the standard RunDiffTests harness. This ensures file overwrites don't corrupt the comparison while keeping the test structure consistent with the framework's expectations.\n\n      -\n\nThe reference binary is found via `exec.LookPath(\"sponge\")` without the g-prefix, since these moreutils don't conflict with macOS built-ins. I'll follow the TestMain pattern from the existing cat and wc tests: build the Go binary to a temp directory, locate the reference binary, run the tests, and clean up gracefully if either binary is missing. For locale handling, I need to check what the preconditions specify.\n\nSetting the environment to `LC_ALL=C` across all test cases to ensure consistent behavior regardless of locale settings. The acceptance criteria require implementing all 8 sponge test cases from the spec, and for file-output tests, I'll run each binary in isolated temp directories and compare the resulting files. I'm checking the spec against the constraints and noticing the requirements count is slightly high at 9 when the target is 5-8. I can consolidate a few related requirements together to bring it down without losing important details. I'm reconsidering the grouping—combining small stdin, empty stdin, and soak-before-write into one requirement feels forced since they're testing different scenarios. Instead, I'll keep them as separate file-output test cases under a single requirement, then organize the rest as passthrough mode, append mode with two variants, large stdin handling, and permission preservation—that gives me seven requirements that fit the 5-8 target without awkward consolidation. I'm verifying the PRD requirement mappings are correct—each of my task requirements should trace back to exactly one PRD requirement. The differential testing and file-state comparison requirements are clearly accounted for, so I'll update the title to explicitly reference both prd007-sponge R6 and prd001-testutils R5, then write out the final YAML.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":113245,"cache_read_input_tokens":18565,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":113245},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29641d3f-feeb-4aee-b1a5-c0cd56516b10","uuid":"0282e3cc-8d96-4586-9986-786c01804f95"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0189VR8PC7VhiDy3GVrB5q9n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development. It derives formal specs from C source code of GNU utilities, generates Go implementations from those specs, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing. The goal is to characterize where the AI code generation methodology holds and where it breaks.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). `cmd/` holds per-utility implementations, `pkg/testutils/` provides the differential testing harness, `pkg/sys/` wraps platform-divergent syscalls, `pkg/format/` handles output formatting. Build via Mage targets; verification via `go test` comparing Go binaries against Homebrew GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Current Implementation**: Source code exists for `pkg/testutils` (difftest.go, normalize.go), `cmd/ts` (main.go, ts_test.go), `cmd/cat` (main.go, cat_test.go), `cmd/wc` (main.go, wc_test.go), and `cmd/sponge` (main.go only — **sponge_test.go is missing**). `pkg/sys/` and `pkg/format/` have PRDs but no implementation code yet.\n\n**Current Release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) has all code artifacts present (ts implementation + tests + testutils). The next clear gap is in rel01.1: `cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go` does not exist, while all other rel01.1 utilities (wc, cat) already have their differential test files.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nrel01.0 has all code artifacts in place. The earliest actionable code gap is `cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go` in rel01.1. This is the single missing test file across all four rel01.x utilities. It depends on `pkg/testutils` (already implemented) and `cmd/sponge/main.go` (already implemented). No other code must be built first.\n\nSponge tests are uniquely challenging because sponge's primary output is a file, not stdout. The standard `RunDiffTests` harness runs both binaries in the same working directory, which means the reference binary would overwrite the Go binary's output file. File-output test cases need a custom helper that runs each binary in its own isolated directory.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/sponge differential tests -- prd007-sponge R6, prd001-testutils R5\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd007-sponge.yaml (R6 differential testing requirements)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (R1-R3 harness API, R5 file-state comparison)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel01.1.yaml (sponge test case definitions -- 8 cases)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.1-uc003-sponge.yaml (use case flow and success criteria)\n      - cmd/sponge/main.go (implementation under test)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (harness: DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests, FileExpectation, executeBinary)\n      - pkg/testutils/normalize.go (NormalizeFunc, ComposeNormalizers)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (pattern reference for TestMain, fixture setup, and RunDiffTests usage)\n      - cmd/wc/wc_test.go (pattern reference for file-based test fixtures with WorkDir)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Differential tests for cmd/sponge exercising all 8 sponge test cases from test-rel01.1.yaml\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Implement TestMain following the pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go: build the Go sponge\n          binary into a temp directory via \"go build -o <path> .\", locate the Homebrew reference\n          binary \"sponge\" (brew install moreutils) via exec.LookPath(\"sponge\"), and set\n          package-level goBinary and refBinary variables. Clean up the temp directory after\n          m.Run(). Per prd007-sponge R6 and sources.yaml (moreutils utilities are not g-prefixed).\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement a runFileDiffTest helper function for file-output test cases. The standard\n          RunDiffTests harness runs both binaries in the same working directory, so the reference\n          binary would overwrite the Go binary's output file. The helper must: (a) create two\n          isolated temp directories with identical fixture files via a setupFixture callback,\n          (b) run the Go binary in directory 1 with the given stdin, args, and env using\n          exec.CommandContext with a 10-second timeout, (c) run the reference binary in directory\n          2 with identical inputs, (d) compare stdout and stderr from both binaries byte-for-byte,\n          (e) compare exit codes, (f) read the output file from both directories and compare their\n          content byte-for-byte, (g) on divergence report: args, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes),\n          both file contents, both stdouts, both stderrs, and both exit codes. Per prd007-sponge\n          R6.1 and prd001-testutils R5.1-R5.2.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Implement three basic file-output test cases using runFileDiffTest:\n          (a) sponge_small_stdin_to_file -- generate 100 lines (\"1\\n\" through \"100\\n\") as stdin,\n          args [\"outfile.txt\"], no fixture setup needed. Traces prd007-sponge R1.1, R1.2, R2.1.\n          (b) sponge_soak_before_write -- create fixture data.txt containing \"line1\\nline2\\nline3\\n\"\n          in both directories, stdin is the same content, args [\"data.txt\"]. Output file must not\n          be empty or truncated. Traces prd007-sponge R1.1, R2.5.\n          (c) sponge_empty_stdin -- empty stdin ([]byte{}), args [\"empty_out.txt\"], no fixture.\n          Output file should be zero bytes. Traces prd007-sponge R1.1, R2.1.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Implement sponge_passthrough_stdout test case using RunDiffTests from pkg/testutils\n          (standard stdout comparison). Stdin is []byte(\"hello world\\n\"), args is nil (no output\n          filename). Both binaries should write stdin content to stdout. Per test-rel01.1.yaml\n          sponge_passthrough_stdout, tracing prd007-sponge R4.1, R4.3.\n      - id: R5\n        text: |\n          Implement two append-mode test cases using runFileDiffTest:\n          (a) sponge_append_mode -- create fixture existing.txt containing \"original line\\n\" in\n          both directories. Stdin \"appended line\\n\", args [\"-a\", \"existing.txt\"]. Output files\n          should contain \"original line\\nappended line\\n\". Traces prd007-sponge R3.1, R3.3.\n          (b) sponge_append_mode_no_existing_file -- no fixture, stdin \"new content\\n\", args\n          [\"-a\", \"newfile.txt\"]. Output files should contain \"new content\\n\". Traces\n          prd007-sponge R3.2.\n      - id: R6\n        text: |\n          Implement sponge_large_stdin test case using runFileDiffTest: generate a payload of\n          1,048,576+ bytes using bytes.Repeat of a known pattern as stdin, args [\"large_out.txt\"],\n          no fixture. Compare output file content from both directories. This exercises the\n          temp-file spill path. Per test-rel01.1.yaml sponge_large_stdin, tracing prd007-sponge\n          R1.3, R1.4.\n      - id: R7\n        text: |\n          Implement sponge_output_file_exists_mode test case using runFileDiffTest: create fixture\n          protected.txt with os.WriteFile using mode 0640 and content \"old content\\n\" in both\n          directories. Stdin \"new content\\n\", args [\"protected.txt\"]. Compare output file content\n          from both directories AND verify the file mode is 0640 in both directories using\n          os.Stat. Per test-rel01.1.yaml sponge_output_file_exists_mode, tracing prd007-sponge\n          R2.3.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Per prd007-sponge R6.1: \"Differential tests must compare the content of the output file\n          (not stdout) after both the Go binary and the reference binary have exited.\" The standard\n          RunDiffTests harness runs both binaries sequentially in the same working directory, which\n          means the reference binary would overwrite the Go binary's output file. File-output test\n          cases use a custom runFileDiffTest helper that creates two isolated temp directories --\n          one per binary -- with identical fixtures, runs each binary in its own directory, and\n          compares the output files from both directories.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Per test-rel01.1.yaml preconditions: \"each test creates an isolated temp directory\" and\n          \"Comparison is on output file content (prd001-testutils R5) for tests with an output\n          filename. Passthrough mode tests compare stdout byte-for-byte.\" Use RunDiffTests from\n          pkg/testutils for the passthrough test only; use runFileDiffTest for all seven\n          file-output tests.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          Per sources.yaml moreutils section: \"Installs without g-prefix (ts, sponge, pee, vidir,\n          chronic) because these utilities do not conflict with any macOS built-ins.\" The reference\n          binary is located via exec.LookPath(\"sponge\"), not \"gsponge\".\n      - id: D4\n        text: |\n          Follow the TestMain pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go: build Go binary into os.MkdirTemp,\n          locate reference binary via exec.LookPath, run m.Run(), clean up with os.RemoveAll. Tests\n          skip gracefully with t.Skip when either binary is unavailable.\n      - id: D5\n        text: |\n          Per test-rel01.1.yaml preconditions: \"All tests use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent\n          divergence.\" Set baseEnv to []string{\"LC_ALL=C\"} and pass it to all test cases.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: |\n          All 8 sponge test cases from test-rel01.1.yaml are implemented: sponge_small_stdi